.TH SPNAV-ONSHAPE-BRIDGE 1 "September 2026" "spnav-onshape-bridge 0.1.6" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
spnav-onshape-bridge \- bridge spacenavd 3D-mouse events to Onshape's browser 3Dconnexion API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spnav-onshape-bridge
[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIIP\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-state\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-sensitivity\fR \fIFACTOR\fR]
.br
.B spnav-onshape-bridge
\fB\-\-doctor\fR
.br
.B spnav-onshape-bridge
\fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spnav-onshape-bridge
lets Onshape (and other web applications that use the same 3Dconnexion
browser API) drive their camera from a
.BR spacenavd (1)
controlled 3D mouse on Linux, in both Chromium and Firefox.
.PP
It listens on a loopback TLS/WebSocket endpoint that mimics the local
service the proprietary Windows 3Dconnexion driver normally provides,
translates
.B spacenavd
motion and button events into the WAMP-over-WebSocket protocol Onshape's
client expects, and rejects any WebSocket upgrade whose
.B Origin
header is not on its allow-list. It generates its own per-installation TLS
certificate rather than shipping or reusing one.
.PP
Setup requires steps beyond simply running the daemon (trusting the
generated certificate, loading a small browser extension or userscript);
see
.B \-\-doctor
below and the project's README.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-host\fR \fIIP\fR
Address to listen on. Defaults to 127.51.68.120, the address Onshape's
client is hard-coded to probe.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR
TCP port to listen on, 1\-65535. Defaults to 8181.
.TP
\fB\-\-state\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Directory used to store the generated CA and leaf TLS certificates and
keys. Defaults to \fB$STATE_DIRECTORY\fR when run as the shipped systemd
\-\-user service, otherwise \fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/spnav-onshape-bridge\fR (or
\fB~/.local/share/spnav-onshape-bridge\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-sensitivity\fR \fIFACTOR\fR
Motion speed multiplier, 0.01\-10. Defaults to 0.35. A value of 1
corresponds to the original, unscaled motion mapping.
.TP
\fB\-\-doctor\fR
Run a self-check of the local setup (spacenavd connectivity, certificate
presence, whether the certificate is trusted by the system NSS database
and any Firefox profiles) and exit. Intended as the first troubleshooting
step; does not start the daemon.
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on a clean exit (including a successful
.BR \-\-help ),
non-zero if
.B \-\-doctor
found a problem, argument parsing failed, or the daemon could not bind its
listening socket or reach spacenavd.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $STATE_DIRECTORY
or
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/spnav-onshape-bridge
CA and leaf TLS certificate/key pairs generated on first run.
.TP
.I /usr/share/spnav-onshape-bridge/nss-trust-install.sh
Imports the generated CA certificate into the system NSS database and any
Firefox profiles, so browsers trust the daemon's TLS endpoint without a
manual per-site exception.
.TP
.I /usr/share/spnav-onshape-bridge/browser-extension/
Chromium extension / Firefox userscript that makes Onshape probe for a
local 3Dconnexion driver on Linux.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
This daemon accepts connections from any local process able to reach its
loopback port and parses untrusted WebSocket/HTTP/JSON input from it. The
Origin allow-list is the only barrier preventing an arbitrary local web
page from reading 3D-mouse motion or issuing camera RPCs; it is not
intended to resist an already-compromised browser or a local attacker with
code execution as the invoking user. See
.B SECURITY.md
in the project source for the full threat model and how to report a
vulnerability.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR spacenavd (1)
.PP
Full documentation, including the certificate trust and browser extension
setup steps that cannot be completed by this program alone:
.br
.I /usr/share/doc/spnav-onshape-bridge/README.md
.SH AUTHOR
Sebastian Polster
